          "settings": {
            "heading": "Frequently Asked Questions",
            "description": "",
            "text_alignment": "left",
            "faq_1_question": "What are compounds like BPC-157, CJC-1295, and Ipamorelin used for?",
            "faq_1_answer": "<p>At Trinity Cell Sciences, we supply research-grade materials such as BPC-157, CJC-1295, and Ipamorelin that are commonly utilized in scientific settings to study cellular repair, signaling pathways, and hormone-related mechanisms. These compounds are intended strictly for laboratory research and analytical use.</p>",
            "faq_2_question": "How does Trinity Cell Sciences ensure purity and quality?",
            "faq_2_answer": "<p>Every batch undergoes advanced analytical verification, including HPLC and mass spectrometry testing, to confirm identity, purity, and consistency. Our commitment to precision ensures researchers receive dependable, high-integrity materials every time.</p>",
            "faq_3_question": "Who are Trinity Cell Sciences products designed for?",
            "faq_3_answer": "<p>Our materials are developed exclusively for qualified researchers, laboratories, and institutions. Trinity Cell Sciences is dedicated to supporting scientific advancement—not consumer or personal use.</p>",
            "faq_4_question": "What is the difference between BPC-157 and TB-500?",
            "faq_4_answer": "<p>BPC-157 is widely studied for its role in localized cellular repair and regeneration processes, while TB-500 (Thymosin Beta-4 fragment) is commonly researched for broader systemic recovery, cell migration, and tissue remodeling applications.</p>",
            "faq_5_question": "Does Trinity Cell Sciences provide Certificates of Analysis (COAs)?",
            "faq_5_answer": "<p>Yes. Every product includes a Certificate of Analysis (COA) for full transparency. This documentation verifies purity, composition, and batch-specific testing results—ensuring traceability and research confidence.</p>",
            "faq_6_question": "How should research materials be stored for maximum stability?",
            "faq_6_answer": "<p>To maintain optimal integrity, store materials in a cool, dry, and dark environment. After reconstitution, refrigeration is typically recommended depending on the compound. Proper storage helps preserve consistency and reliability in research outcomes.</p>",
